THE STANDARD -- MIL-STD-188-113 as IRIG 106-15 App. F reproduces it
  5.5 quotes the compression ratio between the syllabic filter outputs at
  30 % and 0 % run-of-threes.  cvsd.py steps linearly in the filtered
  run-of-threes duty, so a ratio R quoted that way is 1 + (R - 1)/0.30 full
  scale:

    12:1 at 30 %  =  31.5 dB full scale
    16:1 at 30 %  =  34.2 dB full scale
    21:1 at 30 %  =  36.6 dB full scale
    the previous mf8 law, 30 dB full scale  =  10.2:1 at 30 %  (below the 12:1 minimum)
    mf8.BASE, 34.2 dB full scale  =  16.1:1 at 30 %

  Table F-1: the decoder reference patterns, decoded (800 Hz, repeating).
    16k  DB492       specified  0 % run-of-threes, measured  0.0 %
    16k  FB412       specified 30 % run-of-threes, measured 30.0 %
    32k  DB54924AB6  specified  0 % run-of-threes, measured  0.0 %
    32k  FDAA10255E  specified 30 % run-of-threes, measured 30.0 %

  At one fixed step the two patterns do not give one output: the 30 %
  pattern has longer runs, so it swings further per step.
    16k, integrator 1 ms: 30 % pattern / 0 % pattern = 1.86 (5.4 dB)
    16k, integrator 3 ms: 30 % pattern / 0 % pattern = 1.86 (5.4 dB)
    32k, integrator 1 ms: 30 % pattern / 0 % pattern = 1.90 (5.6 dB)
    32k, integrator 3 ms: 30 % pattern / 0 % pattern = 1.90 (5.6 dB)
    3.9 of the standard implies the same thing: 16:1 in step and a 0.48
    amplitude for the 30 % step on the 0 % pattern, so 2.08 (6.4 dB).

  So the standard contradicts itself.  Table F-1 wants the two outputs
  24 +-2 dB apart; 5.5 wants the steps 12:1 to 21:1 apart, which with the
  pattern factor above puts the outputs about 27-32 dB apart.  No decoder
  meets both.  The two readings, as cvsd.py laws with 188-113 timing:

                                   full scale  5.5 ratio  F-1 16k  F-1 32k
    5.5 reading (mf8.BASE)            34.2 dB     16.1:1   29.5 dB   29.7 dB
    Table F-1 reading, 16k            28.4 dB      8.6:1   24.0 dB   24.2 dB
    Table F-1 reading, 32k            28.1 dB      8.4:1   23.8 dB   24.0 dB
    previous mf8 law                  30.0 dB     10.2:1   25.5 dB   25.7 dB
    Table F-1 passes at 22-26 dB; 5.5 passes at 12:1 to 21:1.

  The reference level.  0 dBm0 is the 30 % pattern's output, which is set
  by the slew.  Per 1000 units of slew, mf8.BASE:
    16k: 0 dBm0 = 0.0343 full scale; mf8.SLEW 7000 puts nominal speech at -4.1 dBm0
    32k: 0 dBm0 = 0.0321 full scale; mf8.SLEW 12000 puts nominal speech at -8.2 dBm0
  Equal within a fraction of a dB: the standard holds the slew, not the step,
  across its two rates.  mf8.SLEW raises it with rate instead, which is a
  level alignment -- scaling the slew is scaling the input.

